WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:01.154
Welcome.

2
00:00:01.154 --> 00:00:06.464
In this chapter we build the full weaning and extubation picture.

3
00:00:06.464 --> 00:00:09.731
We wean the patient, not the ventilator.

4
00:00:09.731 --> 00:00:16.430
That means the best predictor of success is improvement of the underlying disease.

5
00:00:16.430 --> 00:00:39.467
We will walk through the readiness screen, the four groups of weaning criteria, and then every classic bedside parameter: sensorium, chest film, blood counts, MIP, vital capacity, RSBI, minute ventilation, blood gas and oxygenation thresholds, hemodynamics, and pulmonary mechanics.

6
00:00:39.467 --> 00:00:47.065
Then we tackle the spontaneous breathing trial, secretions and cough, and the cuff leak test.

7
00:00:47.065 --> 00:00:53.600
Keep this principle front and center: no single value decides weaning readiness.

8
00:00:55.000 --> 00:01:02.381
Before any weaning parameter is measured, the patient must pass a readiness screen.

9
00:01:02.381 --> 00:01:04.693
The deck lists five items.

10
00:01:04.693 --> 00:01:11.362
There must be some reversal of the underlying cause of respiratory failure.

11
00:01:11.362 --> 00:01:25.946
Oxygenation must be adequate, meaning P over F at least one hundred fifty to two hundred, PEEP five to eight or less, and FiO2 at or below point four to point five.

12
00:01:25.946 --> 00:01:30.214
The pH must be at or above seven point two five.

13
00:01:30.214 --> 00:01:38.662
The patient must be hemodynamically stable, with no active ischemia or significant hypotension.

14
00:01:38.662 --> 00:01:44.976
And finally the patient must be able to initiate an inspiratory effort.

15
00:01:44.976 --> 00:01:56.625
Hess warns that no single parameter beats an actual spontaneous breathing trial, and over reliance on numbers prolongs ventilation.

16
00:01:58.033 --> 00:02:00.812
Memorize these three definitions.

17
00:02:00.812 --> 00:02:08.644
Weaning success means no ventilatory support for at least forty eight hours after extubation.

18
00:02:08.644 --> 00:02:16.812
Oxygen, bronchodilators, pressure support, and CPAP during that period do not disqualify success.

19
00:02:16.812 --> 00:02:24.222
Weaning in progress means the patient is extubated but still on noninvasive ventilation.

20
00:02:24.222 --> 00:02:32.475
Weaning failure is a failed spontaneous breathing trial, or reintubation within forty eight hours.

21
00:02:32.475 --> 00:02:42.748
Hess adds that extubation failure, meaning the need to reintubate, happens in five to fifteen percent of patients overall.

22
00:02:42.748 --> 00:02:50.158
Keep that number in mind, because reintubation is itself associated with worse outcomes.

23
00:02:51.567 --> 00:02:56.515
Sensorium is the deck's term for level of consciousness.

24
00:02:56.515 --> 00:03:00.669
You check it by asking time, place, and person.

25
00:03:00.669 --> 00:03:04.115
Oriented times three means fully alert.

26
00:03:04.115 --> 00:03:12.510
The Glasgow Coma Scale is called the most objective measure, with scores from three to fifteen.

27
00:03:12.510 --> 00:03:19.227
Use mild thirteen to fifteen, moderate nine to twelve, severe eight or less.

28
00:03:19.227 --> 00:03:25.412
A GCS of eight or less is a trigger to intubate for airway protection.

29
00:03:25.412 --> 00:03:28.329
Why does this matter for weaning?

30
00:03:28.329 --> 00:03:32.217
Vital capacity and MIP are effort dependent.

31
00:03:32.217 --> 00:03:37.784
A heavily sedated patient looks weak when they are only snowed.

32
00:03:37.784 --> 00:03:47.417
Diminished mental status is also a stopping sign during an SBT, so cooperation really is a bedside parameter.

33
00:03:48.833 --> 00:03:50.951
Hess adds practical hooks.

34
00:03:50.951 --> 00:03:57.710
Daily spontaneous awakening trials paired with SBTs shorten time on the ventilator.

35
00:03:57.710 --> 00:04:14.325
Patients who cannot complete four simple tasks, which are opening the eyes, following with the eyes, grasping your hand, and sticking out the tongue, are more than four times as likely to fail extubation.

36
00:04:14.325 --> 00:04:26.052
For sedation, dexmedetomidine, trade name Precedex, provides sedation without respiratory depression, so it can be continued through extubation.

37
00:04:26.052 --> 00:04:38.024
After neuromuscular blockade, confirm recovery with three bedside tests: a five second head lift, a five second tongue protrusion, and a hand grip.

38
00:04:38.024 --> 00:04:47.308
There is no numeric GCS threshold stated for extubation itself; the deck gives only the airway-protection trigger.

39
00:04:48.733 --> 00:04:56.223
The chest film evaluates thoracic structure: lungs, pleural space, catheters, and tubes.

40
00:04:56.223 --> 00:05:04.650
No source gives a CXR cutoff that gates weaning, but the film tells you which problems to look for.

41
00:05:04.650 --> 00:05:16.140
Sudden drops in compliance point to tension pneumothorax, pleural effusion, or the endotracheal tube slipping into a mainstem bronchus.

42
00:05:16.140 --> 00:05:20.736
Gradual drops suggest pneumonia, ARDS, or atelectasis.

43
00:05:20.736 --> 00:05:27.545
The memory trick is: sudden, search for the problem; gradual, treat the disease.

44
00:05:27.545 --> 00:05:37.248
For tube position, chest film or fiberoptic laryngoscopy are the only confirmations of placement above the carina.

45
00:05:37.248 --> 00:05:44.908
The deck sets the tip four to six centimeters above the carina, usually between T2 and T4.

46
00:05:46.333 --> 00:06:00.447
No source states a CBC cutoff that gates weaning, but the complete blood count matters because anemia lowers oxygen carrying capacity even when oxygenation looks normal.

47
00:06:00.447 --> 00:06:19.489
Normals from the clinical data deck: hemoglobin twelve to sixteen grams per deciliter, hematocrit forty to fifty percent, red cells four to six million per cubic millimeter, white cells five to ten thousand per cubic millimeter.

48
00:06:19.489 --> 00:06:27.256
The deck says septic, anemic, or malnourished patients fail weaning even on perfect settings.

49
00:06:27.256 --> 00:06:35.190
The one deck number is: in ventilated patients, correct anemia when hemoglobin falls below ten.

50
00:06:35.190 --> 00:06:47.383
For infection, white count rises with bacterial pneumonia and may fall with viral; VAP shows leukocytosis or leukopenia plus fever and infiltrate.

51
00:06:48.800 --> 00:06:55.770
MIP, also called NIF, measures inspiratory muscle strength, mostly the diaphragm.

52
00:06:55.770 --> 00:07:09.452
The patient inhales against an occluded manometer for about twenty to twenty five seconds using a one way valve, so each breath is more negative than the last.

53
00:07:09.452 --> 00:07:13.324
Remember the sign: more negative is stronger.

54
00:07:13.324 --> 00:07:17.110
Normal is minus eighty to minus one hundred.

55
00:07:17.110 --> 00:07:26.318
The weaning threshold on this course is minus twenty to minus thirty centimeters of water or more negative.

56
00:07:26.318 --> 00:07:30.534
Minus twenty is the floor every source agrees on.

57
00:07:30.534 --> 00:07:38.881
Hess cautions that even minus twenty may not be enough when resistance is high or compliance low.

58
00:07:38.881 --> 00:07:47.400
During neuromuscular blockade, the diaphragm is the last muscle paralyzed and the first to recover.

59
00:07:48.800 --> 00:07:54.919
Vital capacity is the maximum exhalation after a maximum inspiration.

60
00:07:54.919 --> 00:08:05.826
It combines inspiratory reserve, tidal volume, and expiratory reserve, so it reflects both lung volume and muscle strength.

61
00:08:05.826 --> 00:08:13.541
Normal is roughly fifty milliliters per kilogram, around four to five liters in adults.

62
00:08:13.541 --> 00:08:20.635
The weaning threshold on this exam is greater than ten milliliters per kilogram.

63
00:08:20.635 --> 00:08:27.729
Hess and some RCP 203 notes offer fifteen milliliters per kilogram, but use ten.

64
00:08:27.729 --> 00:08:35.444
VC is effort dependent, so sedated or paralyzed patients cannot give you a real number.

65
00:08:35.444 --> 00:08:49.011
At the bedside, let the patient breathe spontaneously for three minutes, attach a respirometer, have them inhale deeply and exhale fully, then reconnect.

66
00:08:49.011 --> 00:08:53.800
Record the best of three slow vital capacity attempts.

67
00:08:55.233 --> 00:09:00.208
The rapid shallow breathing index is rate divided by size.

68
00:09:00.208 --> 00:09:10.329
You take the respiratory frequency in breaths per minute and divide by the average spontaneous tidal volume in liters.

69
00:09:10.329 --> 00:09:16.333
The deck cutoff is less than one hundred breaths per minute per liter.

70
00:09:16.333 --> 00:09:21.480
Hess and many NBRC protocols say less than one hundred five.

71
00:09:21.480 --> 00:09:29.457
Why the units matter: if you divide by milliliters instead of liters, the answer is nonsense.

72
00:09:29.457 --> 00:09:43.266
Rapid shallow breathing hurts because anatomic dead space is unchanged while tidal volume falls, which raises the dead space fraction and the ventilatory demand.

73
00:09:43.266 --> 00:09:47.983
Slow deep breaths are good; fast small breaths are bad.

74
00:09:49.400 --> 00:09:51.483
Let us run two examples.

75
00:09:51.483 --> 00:10:02.072
Example one: the patient breathes thirty eight times per minute with an average tidal volume of three hundred milliliters.

76
00:10:02.072 --> 00:10:05.196
Convert to liters, zero point three.

77
00:10:05.196 --> 00:10:11.705
Divide: thirty eight over zero point three equals one hundred twenty seven.

78
00:10:11.705 --> 00:10:17.347
That is above one hundred, so the index predicts weaning failure.

79
00:10:17.347 --> 00:10:23.249
Example two: minute ventilation ten liters at a rate of twenty five.

80
00:10:23.249 --> 00:10:30.539
Average tidal volume is ten divided by twenty five, which is zero point four liters.

81
00:10:30.539 --> 00:10:37.656
RSBI is twenty five divided by zero point four, which equals sixty two point five.

82
00:10:37.656 --> 00:10:42.169
That is under one hundred, a good weaning candidate.

83
00:10:42.169 --> 00:10:50.675
Trap: if you divide by four hundred milliliters you get zero point zero six, which is meaningless.

84
00:10:52.100 --> 00:11:09.889
The deck normals are: minute ventilation six to ten liters per minute, spontaneous tidal volume five to ten milliliters per kilogram, spontaneous rate twelve to twenty, PaCO2 thirty five to forty five.

85
00:11:09.889 --> 00:11:23.164
For weaning, a minute ventilation above ten liters is a sign of excessive work and poor outcomes, because high demand means a loaded respiratory pump.

86
00:11:23.164 --> 00:11:31.484
The minimum spontaneous tidal volume consistent with success is five milliliters per kilogram.

87
00:11:31.484 --> 00:11:43.520
Hess collects the classic predictors as spontaneous VT five, rate thirty, minute ventilation twelve, VC fifteen, and PImax minus twenty.

88
00:11:43.520 --> 00:11:50.158
Use the deck's ten liter ceiling on this exam, and recognize Hess's twelve.

89
00:11:50.158 --> 00:11:58.300
The NHLBI ARDS protocol accepts VT at or above four milliliters per kilogram during its SBT.

90
00:11:59.733 --> 00:12:08.396
The oxygenation question is: does this patient still need a lot of oxygen and PEEP?

91
00:12:08.396 --> 00:12:11.214
If yes, they are not ready.

92
00:12:11.214 --> 00:12:36.157
The weaning deck lists PaO2 above sixty or SaO2 above ninety on FiO2 of point four or less, P over F at or above one hundred fifty, shunt under twenty percent, and A minus a gradient under three hundred fifty on one hundred percent oxygen.

93
00:12:36.157 --> 00:12:50.873
P over F categories: above three hundred eighty normal, under three hundred mild ARDS, under two hundred ARDS, under one hundred fifty prone.

94
00:12:50.873 --> 00:12:58.283
Shunt over twenty percent must be corrected before any weaning attempt.

95
00:12:58.283 --> 00:13:11.433
During an SBT, keep SpO2 at or above eighty five to ninety, pH at or above seven point three, and PaCO2 rise ten torr or less.

96
00:13:12.867 --> 00:13:15.226
A trap worth repeating.

97
00:13:15.226 --> 00:13:25.380
If the patient hyperventilates and PaCO2 drops during weaning, do not reflexively drop the support.

98
00:13:25.380 --> 00:13:32.457
Low PaCO2 may be driven by hypoxia, pain, anxiety, or wrong settings.

99
00:13:32.457 --> 00:13:41.687
If you reduce support to chase the gas, work of breathing climbs and the patient fatigues.

100
00:13:41.687 --> 00:13:44.046
Find the cause instead.

101
00:13:44.046 --> 00:14:01.380
After neuromuscular blockade, the pre-extubation targets in the deck are PaO2 above eighty and PaCO2 under forty five, with COPD patients compared to their own baseline.

102
00:14:01.380 --> 00:14:15.842
On this exam, follow the deck's P over F of one fifty as the oxygenation screen, and recognize Hess's one hundred fifty to two hundred range.

103
00:14:17.267 --> 00:14:24.672
Hemodynamic stability means no active myocardial ischemia and no significant hypotension.

104
00:14:24.672 --> 00:14:30.164
The weaning deck requires acceptable vital signs before any trial.

105
00:14:30.164 --> 00:14:51.631
During the spontaneous breathing trial, keep heart rate below one twenty to one forty with a change under twenty percent, systolic between ninety and one eighty to two hundred with the same twenty percent limit, and no vasopressors or only low dose pressors.

106
00:14:51.631 --> 00:14:59.120
Clinical signs of failure include tachycardia, hypertension, hypotension, and arrhythmias.

107
00:14:59.120 --> 00:15:01.034
Hess adds a quiet trap.

108
00:15:01.034 --> 00:15:17.842
A patient with marginal left ventricular function may tolerate CPAP because positive pressure unloads the failing heart, and then flash into pulmonary edema after extubation when the pressure comes off.

109
00:15:19.267 --> 00:15:23.080
These three are effort independent.

110
00:15:23.080 --> 00:15:31.470
Static compliance equals tidal volume divided by plateau pressure minus PEEP.

111
00:15:31.470 --> 00:15:39.097
The weaning value is above thirty milliliters per centimeter of water.

112
00:15:39.097 --> 00:15:45.090
Normal intubated adult compliance runs forty to eighty.

113
00:15:45.090 --> 00:15:59.146
Dead space fraction uses the Enghoff modification of the Bohr equation: PaCO2 minus the mean mixed exhaled CO2, divided by PaCO2.

114
00:15:59.146 --> 00:16:03.722
Weaning target is less than sixty percent.

115
00:16:03.722 --> 00:16:16.906
A worked example: PaCO2 forty, PECO2 twenty seven, so thirteen over forty equals zero point three two five, about normal.

116
00:16:16.906 --> 00:16:25.623
Airway resistance equals PIP minus plateau divided by flow in liters per second.

117
00:16:25.623 --> 00:16:34.667
Normal is zero point six to two point four, higher on the vent because of the tube.

118
00:16:36.100 --> 00:16:39.658
The SBT is the single best bedside test.

119
00:16:39.658 --> 00:16:48.376
The deck says up to thirty minutes, because patients who fail do so in the first twenty to thirty.

120
00:16:48.376 --> 00:16:53.001
RCP 203 and Hess allow thirty to one hundred twenty.

121
00:16:53.001 --> 00:17:03.587
Methods have similar success: T piece, pressure support five to eight, CPAP about five, or automatic tube compensation.

122
00:17:03.587 --> 00:17:07.056
SIMV is not a stand alone weaning mode.

123
00:17:07.056 --> 00:17:19.777
Hess's twenty seventeen guideline suggests starting with pressure support of five to eight for patients ventilated more than twenty four hours.

124
00:17:19.777 --> 00:17:27.338
Pass criteria are a normal pattern, adequate gas exchange, and hemodynamic stability.

125
00:17:27.338 --> 00:17:40.325
Failure signs are agitation, diminished mental status, diaphoresis, cyanosis, rising work, tachypnea, accessory muscle use, and abdominal paradox.

126
00:17:41.733 --> 00:17:49.737
A spontaneous respirometer reading shows a minute ventilation of twelve liters at a rate of forty.

127
00:17:49.737 --> 00:17:53.983
Pause and compute the rapid shallow breathing index.

128
00:17:53.983 --> 00:17:58.883
Does the number predict a pass or a fail by the deck cutoff?

129
00:17:59.800 --> 00:18:05.800
[6-second practice pause]

130
00:18:06.300 --> 00:18:13.496
Tidal volume equals twelve divided by forty, which is zero point three liters.

131
00:18:13.496 --> 00:18:20.968
RSBI is forty divided by zero point three, which equals one hundred thirty three.

132
00:18:20.968 --> 00:18:30.655
That is above the deck's cutoff of one hundred and above the NBRC one hundred five, so the patient fails.

133
00:18:30.655 --> 00:18:35.175
Rapid shallow breathing predicts weaning failure.

134
00:18:36.600 --> 00:18:40.655
Passing the SBT answers the breathing question.

135
00:18:40.655 --> 00:18:45.917
Extubation also requires that the patient protect the airway.

136
00:18:45.917 --> 00:19:03.774
RCP 203 says remove the tube only if the patient maintains oxygenation and ventilation without support, is at minimal risk for upper airway obstruction, protects against aspiration, and can clear secretions.

137
00:19:03.774 --> 00:19:08.864
Cough strength is tracked with maximum expiratory pressure.

138
00:19:08.864 --> 00:19:15.076
Normal is sixty to one hundred, and under forty is an ineffective cough.

139
00:19:15.076 --> 00:19:36.125
Hess adds hard numbers: cough peak flow at or below sixty liters per minute multiplies failure risk by five, secretions above two point five milliliters per hour triples risk, and suctioning more than every two hours should preclude extubation.

140
00:19:37.533 --> 00:19:51.514
Before extubating a patient at risk for upper airway swelling, deflate the cuff on positive pressure and listen for a leak around the tube, or measure a leak volume.

141
00:19:51.514 --> 00:20:03.885
Risk factors include traumatic intubation, intubation longer than six days, a large tube, female sex, and reintubation after unplanned extubation.

142
00:20:03.885 --> 00:20:11.511
The test has false positives and negatives, and deflating the cuff raises aspiration risk.

143
00:20:11.511 --> 00:20:22.357
If the test fails, the twenty seventeen ATS and ACCP guideline suggests systemic steroids at least four hours before extubation.

144
00:20:22.357 --> 00:20:30.999
After extubation, if stridor develops give cool humidified oxygen and aerosolized racemic epinephrine.

145
00:20:30.999 --> 00:20:36.083
The most serious complication of extubation is laryngospasm.

146
00:20:37.500 --> 00:20:52.288
Name the deck's weaning threshold for each of the following parameters: static compliance, dead space fraction, vital capacity, maximum inspiratory pressure, and minute ventilation ceiling.

147
00:20:52.288 --> 00:20:56.200
Try to call out each number before the next slide.

148
00:20:57.100 --> 00:21:03.100
[6-second practice pause]

149
00:21:03.600 --> 00:21:05.758
Here are the deck values.

150
00:21:05.758 --> 00:21:12.147
Static compliance greater than thirty milliliters per centimeter of water.

151
00:21:12.147 --> 00:21:15.946
Dead space fraction less than sixty percent.

152
00:21:15.946 --> 00:21:20.868
Vital capacity greater than ten milliliters per kilogram.

153
00:21:20.868 --> 00:21:29.156
Maximum inspiratory pressure minus twenty to minus thirty centimeters of water or more negative.

154
00:21:29.156 --> 00:21:36.322
Minute ventilation ceiling ten liters per minute above which weaning tends to fail.

155
00:21:36.322 --> 00:21:49.100
These are the Chang deck numbers; recognize Hess variants of fifteen for VC and twelve for minute ventilation, but default to the deck on this exam.

156
00:21:50.500 --> 00:22:01.163
Weaning failure comes from three mechanisms: rising airway resistance, falling compliance, and respiratory muscle fatigue.

157
00:22:01.163 --> 00:22:14.274
Resistance climbs with a small tube, secretions, kinking, biting, circuit condensation, or bronchospasm; everything except bronchospasm is mechanical.

158
00:22:14.274 --> 00:22:22.752
A standard endotracheal tube raises work of breathing by fifty four to two hundred forty percent.

159
00:22:22.752 --> 00:22:34.726
Compliance drops suddenly from tension pneumothorax, effusion, or mainstem migration, and gradually from pneumonia, ARDS, or atelectasis.

160
00:22:34.726 --> 00:22:53.868
Fatigue is driven by poor oxygen delivery, electrolyte derangements such as low potassium, phosphate, calcium, and magnesium, undernutrition that depletes diaphragm substrate, and overfeeding that raises CO2 production.

161
00:22:53.868 --> 00:22:58.500
After fatigue, Hess suggests a twenty four hour rest.

162
00:22:59.900 --> 00:23:01.337
Two extra traps.

163
00:23:01.337 --> 00:23:10.498
Diaphragm disuse atrophy can appear in as little as twenty four hours of fully controlled ventilation.

164
00:23:10.498 --> 00:23:19.209
Retrain the diaphragm with short T-tube trials or pressure support, not with prolonged full rest.

165
00:23:19.209 --> 00:23:30.525
Auto-PEEP in COPD raises the pleural pressure the patient must generate to trigger a breath, so it directly delays liberation.

166
00:23:30.525 --> 00:23:45.254
The deck also reminds us that fever and infection raise both oxygen consumption and CO2 production, which increase ventilatory demand independent of lung mechanics.

167
00:23:45.254 --> 00:23:49.475
Fix the drivers, do not just chase the numbers.

168
00:23:50.900 --> 00:23:51.833
To recap.

169
00:23:51.833 --> 00:23:59.920
First screen for reversal of cause, oxygenation, pH, hemodynamics, and effort.

170
00:23:59.920 --> 00:24:12.155
Then check the four groups of parameters: clinical readiness, ventilatory reserve, oxygenation, and pulmonary reserve.

171
00:24:12.155 --> 00:24:37.038
Memorize the deck numbers: RSBI under one hundred, VT at least five, VE ten or less, VC over ten, MIP more negative than minus twenty, P over F at least one fifty, pH at least seven two five, Cst over thirty, VD over VT under sixty percent.

172
00:24:37.038 --> 00:24:42.326
Confirm readiness with an SBT up to thirty minutes.

173
00:24:42.326 --> 00:24:51.761
Then ask the separate airway protection question: cough, secretions, upper airway swelling.

174
00:24:51.761 --> 00:25:01.300
Weaning success is forty eight hours off, and extubation failure is five to fifteen percent.
